# instance/config/memory-supersession.yml — auto-apply posture for the
# memory supersession loop (cabinet/scripts/memory-supersede-apply.py;
# detect on the services.yml row memory-contradictions, Sundays 05:30;
# soak+apply on its own row memory-supersede-apply, Sundays 05:45).
#
# auto_apply:
#   soak  — DEFAULT, per 2026-07-15 Captain ratification of memory Wave 3:
#           propose-only for the first 14 days from the organ's first
#           soak-ledger entry; auto-apply (near-duplicate class ONLY) arms
#           by itself once the ledger shows >= 14 days AND zero UNRESOLVED
#           reversals. At half-soak (day 7+) the organ files ONE Captain
#           heads-up card on the needs ledger (counts + arming date), so
#           arming is never observation-free — and the card's one-taps
#           BIND: approve lets the soak arm, veto HOLDS the gate (posture
#           held-by-captain-veto, re-read from the live needs row every
#           run until a later grant on the same card re-rules it).
#           Contradiction-cue pairs never auto-apply on similarity — they
#           file Captain one-tap cards on the needs ledger, retried each
#           run until a need id is recorded; APPROVING a card (binder
#           grant → needs status approved_pending_apply — for these
#           decision cards the binder approval IS the ruling; nothing
#           outside the organ ever writes "granted" for them) is executed
#           by the organ's next pass once the gate arms (same guarded,
#           reversible UPDATE — the approval waives only the similarity
#           bar), after which the organ closes the need (status granted,
#           the receipt); a veto simply never approves and both rows stay.
#   hold  — propose-only FOREVER: the organ keeps recording would-apply
#           decisions and filing cue cards but never writes superseded_by
#           (Captain-approved cards included — hold outranks everything).
#
# A missing file defaults to soak (the ratified posture). An unrecognized
# value, unparseable yaml, or an EXISTING file that cannot be read fails
# safe to hold — a typo or a broken read must never arm.
#
# Reversal recovery (the latch): any --undo appends a "reversal" entry to
# shared/interfaces/memory-supersession-soak.jsonl and re-blocks arming.
# The ONLY supported re-arm is the Captain ruling
#   python3.12 cabinet/scripts/memory-supersede-apply.py \
#     --resolve-reversals "why the reversal is settled"
# which appends a reversal-resolved marker (only reversals AFTER the latest
# marker block). NEVER truncate or hand-edit the soak ledger to recover:
# consumed-but-open pairs live there. (If surgery ever happens anyway, the
# organ reopens consumed pairs from their proposals-file stamps.)
auto_apply: soak
